 precarious housing conditions and COVID-19 pandemic: peculiarities and prevention strategies.\" European Review for Medical and Pharmacological Sciences. 24.18; 9765-9767.\nFowler, P. J., & Farrell, A. F. (2017). Housing and child well being: implications for research, policy, and practice.\nAmerican Journal of Community Psychology. 60(1-2), 3-8.\n\nGotoff et al. (2024). Public Awareness and Perceptions of Disparities in Maine: Analysis of Survey Findings. A report to\nthe Permanent Commission on the Status of Racial, lndigenous, and Tribal Populations.\n\nMaineHousing. (October 2023). State of Maine Housing Production Needs Study.\n\nlbid.\nm US Census Bureau (2023). Quarterly Vacancy and Homeownership Rates by State and MSA. Table 1. Rental Vacancy\nRates by State: 2005 - present. America's Health Rankings. (2022). Homeownership racial disparity.\n-o@eXp@e/measuresfimeoumcmm clispacit;dME#_\nMaineHousing. (October 2023). State of Maine Housing Production Needs Study. Rothstein, R. (2017). The color of law: A forgotten history ofhow our government segregatedAmerica. Liveright Publishing.\nBachaud, N. (2022). Black mortgage applicants denied 84% more often than white borrowers. Zillow.\n-dmmme-hmm-2o2o_3osm\nMaineHousing. (2023). 2023 Point in time count. Gotoff et al. (2024). Public Awareness and Perceptions of Disparities in Maine: Analysis of Survey Findings. A report to the Permanent Commission on the Status of Racial, lndigenous, and Tribal Populations.\nMaineHousing, Governor's Office of Policy Innovation and the Future, Maine Department of Economic and Community Development. (2023). State of Maine Housing Production Needs Study.\nGotoff et al. (2024). Public Awareness and Perceptions of Disparities in Maine: Analysis of Survey Findings. A report to\nthe Permanent Commission on the Status of Racial, lndigenous, and Tribal Populations.\nBoerma, J., & Karabarbounis, L. (2023). Reparations and persistent racial wealth gaps. NBER Macroeconomic. Annual, 37(1), 171-221.\nSchaeffer, Katherine. (2021). `What's the difference between income and wealth?` And other common questions about economic concepts. Decoded (blag), Pew Research Center. Accessed December 22, 2023. Miller, M. C. (2011). Land and racial wealth inequality. American Economic Review. 101(3), 371-376.\nNovak, D. A. (2014). The wheel of servitude: Black forced labor after slavery. University Press of Kentucky.\nLui, M. (2006). The co/or of wealth: The story behind the US racial wealth divide. The New Press.\nOliver, M., & Shapiro, T. (2013). Blaclr wea/th/white wealth: A new perspective on racial inequality. Routledge. Shapiro, T., Meschede, T., & Osoro, S. (2013). The roots of the widening racial wealth gap: Explaining the Black-white\neconomic divide. Research and Policy Brief. lnstitute on Assets and Social Policy, Brandeis University.\nUS Department of Labor. (2020). Earnings disparities by race and ethnicity.\n\nUS Department of Labor. (2020). Earnings disparities by sex.\n\nUS Census Bureau. (2023). Median Household lncome in the past 12 months (in 2022 inflation adjusted dollars). American Community Survey, ACS 1-Year Estimates Subject Tables, Table B19013.\n\nUS Census Bureau, US Department of Commerce. (2023). Median lncome in the Past 12 Months (in 2023 InflationAdjusted Dollars). American Community Survey, ACS 1-Year Estimates Subject Tables, Table S1903.\n.\nKalt, J., Medford, A.B & Taylor, .l.B. (2022). Economic and Social /mpacts of Restrictions on the Applicability of Federal\nIndian Policies to the Wabanalci Nations in Maine. The Harvard Project on American Indian Economic Development.\nPerry, A., Stephens, H., & Donoghoe, M. (2024). Black wealth is increasing, but so is the racial wealth gap. Brookings Institute.\nH America's Health Rankings. (2022). Homeownership racial disparity.\ns-owexduflmeasures/Mmeomflmb\nBaradaran, M. (2017). The color of money: Black banks and the racial wealth gap. Harvard University Press.\nAladangady, A., & Forde, A. (2021, October 22). Wealth lnequality and the Racial Wealth Gap. FEDS Notes.\ne-gm/ecomesmmesLfedsmoiesbueahhflnequaliiy-andAbe--mcialauealib;gap--202DQ2Zbiml\nBurgard, S.A., & Lin, K.Y. (2013). Bad jobs, bad health? How work and working conditions contribute to health\ndisparities. American Behavioral Scientist. 57(8).\nPrice, R.H, Choi, J.N., & Vinkur, A.D. (2002). Links in the chain of adversity following job loss: How financial strain and loss of\npersonal control lead to depression, impaired functioning, and poor health. Joumal of Occupational Health and Psychology. 7(4).\n25", "10058322": "Statement for LD2149 for the\nHousing & Economic Development Committee\nHello committee members, my name is Sarah Giles-Gardner. I have Dysarthria, (a speech disorder that affects the way I talk) FND, and a Covid-19 vaccine injury. Please be patient and let me know if you need me to repeat something. I live at 17 Pine Haven Street in Saco Maine, and I would like to submit a statement in support for LD2149 for the Housing & Economic Development Committee on Thursday January 22, 2025.\nAs the Blue Haven community moves forward with this mediation process that was put into place by the State of Maine to help protect both residents and businesses. This might have started off as a great idea, however so far it's done nothing, but add more stress and strain on already a difficult time for residents who are on a fixed income and are disabled, veterans, single moms, elderly, and people are scared to speak up about what is happening around us in our mobile home parks. These large corporations have done nothing but steal our hard-earned money and continue to put none of it back into our community. Even when they know we have large capital improvements that need to be completed. So, with the CPI plus 1% rent increase they know that they can charge us again even more next year if they present these projects. However, there is no guarantee that these projects will ever be completed by them. 2 years ago they submitted our project to the city and have raised our rents the last 5 years and according to the city of Saco planning board it's on administrative pause because the company never responded or even started the process with the planning board to get our projects done for city sewer, but yet we have a piece of paper that has plans on it, but no set plans are being made currently at Saco city hall. However, according to the owners they will be starting this project soon. They have no intentions of doing it at all. They believe they are above the laws that you and the city create and no matter how many laws we find that they break, we still don't feel like we are getting support from any of the laws that you write because they always find loopholes and work their way around and interpret in whatever way works for them to benefit them.\nThroughout this mediation process it would have been nice to have a resource or place to go for questions about this process. We should also have been given an attorney for legal advice as they have several attorneys that were not disclosed to us until closer to the mediation date. Also, we tried to reach out to Pine Tree Legal at the beginning of all of this and they could not help us either. Luckily, we all pooled together and got just enough for an attorney for this mediation process, but we still need a lot more help from you. These companies have posted illegal street signs according to city of Saco and who is going to make sure that this gets enforced or corrected for safety's sake. This company just plans to sell us amongst themselves and leave us with all these problems, some of which they created. We used to have the correct street signs with Pine Haven\n\nStreet on them, now they just say Pine Haven. Unfortunately, unless someone listens to us and decides to put a stop to this company, they will continue to do the same illegal practices repeatedly with someone else. Where do you stand, with the people or the corrupt companies? It's your choice.\nMy husband also wrote a letter to the Attorney General's office because upon request of looking into my lease, they could not provide me with one. They requested me to go sign a new lease because they say that I'm a tenant at will. When I read their new lease, they require me to be responsible for adding an insurance policy with a minimum of 300,000 on my home (which is worth around 121,700, so why do I have to put more insurance than is needed). They purchased the park for 12 million and now this year was appraised for 11.3 million, until we found errors at Saco city hall and now it's worth is 10.9 million dollars. We have had constant concerns and problems ever since this new company took over 5 years ago and the lack of work reflects their dedication to our communities in Maine. Where is the oversight for these companies? We are up against a 67.9-million-dollar company right now. Any support is greatly appreciated. Donna Bailey has been inspiring role in our community, but again it's not enough we all need your help to create stronger language to protect the people of these communities against these large corporations who will continue unless you help us to enforce the laws, not just create them. All these laws and languages are just pieces of paper to them, and we are seen as a commodity to them. Please help to change this corruption.\nI thought as we approached this mediation things would be more transparent and the only thing that we keep finding is more lies and no justification for the $145.00 increase to my rent. LD1016 was created so that when the park was sold then the buyer would have to pay the 10,000 fee per lot, however, to circumvent around this law and bypass the right of first refusal law to sell to us the residents first they are just transferring ownership underneath us amongst themselves through a real estate transfer tax. So now the buyer doesn't have to pay the 10,000 per lot fee, nor do they have to offer it to us. They also don't have to disclose it to us. Even though they continue to inform us that we are not for sale, we have found evidence to override their claims.\nThey have also given us a breakdown of what they proposed our lot fee to be going towards and $100.00 of this fee is The LD1016 that is being paid by the residents and not the buyer. I can see justification for some rent increases; however, this should not be part of our rent increase. These large corporations come in and find ways to charge us more either legally or illegally and they get away with it. I'm strapped for every penny I have right now. Anything you can do to be able to protect residents from these large corporate moguls would be the difference between having affordable housing still or becoming homeless. This is our reality and it's happening in real time, and this should be your top priority so we can continue to be productive members of our community and committee. We have created a committee to help other vulnerable residents who are\n\nscared to speak up against them. Thank you for your time today and I hope my voice can be heard. I would like to see Maine people continuing to help Mainer's who have lived here all their lives. We just want a safe place to call home that's affordable.\n\nSarah Giles-Gardner Saco LD 2149\nI have uploaded my statement. Thanks Sarah", "196757": "$t\n126 Route 1\n\nMann Faetu red Homes\n\nScarborough, ME 04074\n\nOpposition to LD 2149\n\nHonorable Members of the Committee on Housing and Economic Development\n\nThank you for the opportunity to address this very important bill. We are the only State in the\n'\nNation to confiscate $10,000 per site for manufactured housing communities that were\n\ndeveloped with no funds provided by any government entity, no tax abatement, and no\nsupport. Our company was started 84 years ago by my parents. l've worked at the company for over 50 years. Through the years we have fought for the right to develop our land with the Town government, the neighbors, and sometimes even the State. You are now stating that \"mobile home parks\" provide the only non subsidized affordable housing in the State. For that we have\nbeen crucified here, on television, and in the news for the last couple of years. We are not all \"Out of State\" big businesses that gouge the tenants. We are citizens of Maine, family members\n\nwho have worked very hard to build businesses that provide affordable housing to residents of\n\nour State.\n\n.\n\nls it possible that the \"$10,000 fee\" on the sale of our 2 communities amounting to over 4 million dollars is too much? Perhaps you should consider a cap on this onerous fee. l understand the $10,000 per site fee is already in law. But please consider the following:\n\n`\nSec.4 2 Exemptions:\n\n2. Any family business or family held corporation that developed a manufactured housing l/}JL/community without government funding and has been in existence for over 10 years\n1/\nE. An entity that, together with any affiliated or related entities has a net worth of less than $50\nmillion. The limit must be raised from 50 million. _$50O million is more realistic. Because of my\nparents' foresight and hard work as well as that of the remaining family, 200 acres of land on\nRoute 1 in Scarborough has appreciated substantially and we should not be penalized.\n\nExamples of publicly held purchasers of communities are:\n\nOffice (207) ssa-2512 Fax (207) ass-sose statemanufacturedhomes.com\n\nUMH formally called United Mobile Homes is valued at over 1 Billion Dollars\nSun Communities is worth over 15 Billion Dollars.\n(2) any entity owning or controlling 10% or more of the outstanding voting interests. Many family or small businesses are owned or controlled by a limited number of people. Should they\nbe penalized?\n@)_Any officer, director or general partner of the entity. Most family owned or relatively small businesses have limited officers, directors or general partners. You are limiting most of us from\ninvesting in other communities without the penalty of $10,000 per site.\nSection 7 BBB : Most taxpayers do not own 50% in a qualified property because of the\nQ\ninheritance tax laws. This should be changed to an ownership interest of 35 % or less.\nSection 8 KK Including \"Cooperative affordable housing` corporation organized under Title 13\nchapter 85\" Consumer Cooperatives, expands the definition of \"resident owned communities\"\nbeyond what most of the citizens of the State of Maine understood. Title 13 Chapter 85 expands the allowable organizations well beyond the definition of non-profits most of us understand.\nBefore you -vote in favor of LD 2149 please review Title 13 Chapter 85. If you want to give our\ninvestments and hard work to others, be sure the recipient is a truly \" Resident Owned\nCommunity\"\nPlease allow me to make the suggestion that this bill be tabled and the issues studied further. We are the people that built the \"only affordable non-subsidized housing in the State\", not the enemy. We deserve fair treatment too.\n\nRes\n\n~\nully/\n\n/Z44/5,4-K4: aw': e'./ ~\n\n\"\"'i\" _.t...�'\"\n\nTheresa Desfosses State Manufactured Homes, Inc 126 Route #1\nScarborough, ME 04074\n\n(207) 883-2512", "196758": "I\nSt\n126 Route 1\n\nrcd\nlrV{g:I11lg;;CU1\n\nScarborough, ME 04074\n\nOpposition to LD 2149\nDear Senator Curry, Representative Gere and members of the Housing Committee,\nWriting in OPPOSITION to LD 2149\n_\nMy name is Tinamarie Smith. I am Vice President of State Manufactured Homes, Inc. which is\nlocated in Scarborough. We are a family owned and operated company, started by my grandparents in 1944. We own two large manufactured housing communities in Scarborough.\n_LD 2149 and the \"corrective language\" it contains to further expand on last year's LD 1016 will\nfurther tie the hands of privately owned businesses that are successful from purchasing other\nmanufactured housing communities in our state. With language such as entities with a net\nworth of 50 Mil dollars or more or persons who own more than 10% of the voting rights of an affiliated company are not entitled to purchase other existing communities without triggering the $10,000 per site fee is very difficult to live with. A family business such as ours is worth over\n50 million just from the appreciation of the land and our community in the past 84 years. If you want involvement from a family business it's important to give us the ability to purchase other manufactured housing communities without the significant penalty of $10,000 per site. lt gives the ROC entity an unfair advantage over the very thing that many in this very room said they did not want to do, and that is remove family ownership from the marketplace.\n\n'\nTinamarie Smith\nState Manufactured Homes Inc\n126 Route #1\nScarborough, ME 04074\nOffice (207) 883-2512 Fax (207) 883-8089 statemanufacturedhomes.com"}